                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "f51fe37c-a245-314a-a387-f57ffb8393a1",
                        "entry_id": "6141b7da-739d-3d8e-8ae4-d32f5d2a1f88",
                        "glosses": [
                            "To coax (a rabbit) from a burrow by poking a stick down the hole and moving it about; to delve into a hole in order to locate an animal."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "UK",
                            "regional"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "e4fbcdfb-1821-401b-bb1d-d387cd3e953b",
                                "sense_id": "f51fe37c-a245-314a-a387-f57ffb8393a1",
                                "text": "1833, \"Javelin,\" Two Days with the East Sussex, in Sporting Magazine, 2nd Series, Volume 6, page 236,\nIn concluding my remarks on the East Sussex Hounds, I must observe, that perhaps they have the worst and most difficult country in the world to hunt through, intersected with immense woods, and slotted with some thousands of sheep, besides being naturally a very cold-scenting soil; consequently the hounds puggle more than is pleasing to the eye, have little or no dash, but are very patient and very perfect in working up to their fox, and are probably much surer killers than any other hounds […] ."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "d888030f-09e0-30f8-930d-912f2207abec",
                        "entry_id": "6141b7da-739d-3d8e-8ae4-d32f5d2a1f88",
                        "glosses": [
                            "To poke around a hole with a stick, as to explore, remove obstacles, etc."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "UK",
                            "regional"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "3e914096-00f8-4077-b88e-c45598056c61",
                                "sense_id": "d888030f-09e0-30f8-930d-912f2207abec",
                                "text": "1828, \"Philip Fidget,\" The Confessions of Philip Fidget, in The Spirit and Manners of the Age, New Series, Volume 1, page 417,\nI am no sooner seated by a fire, but my hand instinctively fumbles after the poker, and I proceed to puggle the glowing embers, until (except timely warning prevent) I have displaced the whole into the fender."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "cbbb4348-55a7-4fdc-a517-18d8a1870294",
                                "sense_id": "d888030f-09e0-30f8-930d-912f2207abec",
                                "text": "He puggled around in the oil cavity with his little lead pencil until it slipped out of his fingers and went down into the oil cavity."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
